Moving between countries means solving dozens of problems that depend on each other: leaving a home, setting up a new one, work and income, health coverage, identity documents, tax administration, shipping, selling things, and the people involved. When a travel date or work assumption shifts, it ripples into lease notice periods, coverage start dates, sale timing, and weekly cash needs. The hard part is keeping the whole picture consistent while the plan moves.

I used an AI assistant, working from a folder of plain files, to help coordinate one real international move. Then I turned the method into [Relocation Playbook](https://github.com/korabeland/relocation-playbook), an open-source kit with a [process case study](https://github.com/korabeland/relocation-playbook/blob/main/docs/case-study.md). Every example in the kit and the case study is invented. Both leave out the names, places, employers, providers, money figures, and outcome of the real move. Neither claims measured time savings or any missed deadlines prevented.

The first system split the work by job: `PLAN.md` for strategy, a Notion board for tasks, shared folders for research and documents, and a short memory index. The assistant compared administrative, financial, healthcare, and shipping options and drafted checklists and tables. It set up a scenario budget in four buckets (exit, travel, one-time setup, and recurring costs) and kept a household sale's listing dates separate from its handover dates. One person drove the assistant, and the rest of the household joined reviews in plain language.

Then the plan changed. It moved from waiting on events to working back from a fixed date, and later took in changing work and coverage assumptions. The first setup broke in three specific ways:

- **A stale shared agenda.** The shared agenda only changed at weekly reviews. Solo sessions in between changed the plan with no wrap-up, so the agenda fell behind the real work.
- **Scattered facts and slow starts.** Dates in the memory index drifted away from the task board, and reading the board page by page at the start of each session was slow.
- **Overconfident labels and conflicting rules.** A file named "verified facts" held an unconfirmed inference. Approval wording conflicted across files, and the assistant's own gap audit reported a topic as missing when it had only been covered lightly.

So I rebuilt the foundation around small structured records in `state/` and one approval contract in `AGENTS.md`. Each fact now records whether it is confirmed, an assumption, or a recommendation, plus its source, when it was checked, and a date to check it again. Deadlines are marked hard (legal or booked) or soft (self-imposed), and sit alongside open decisions, a prioritised research queue, and a cached snapshot of the task board. The session-end workflow now runs after every solo or shared session. It builds the agenda from the records instead of from conversation memory, and it flags anything outside the folder that it did not update.

The first reconciliation after the redesign caught a wrong date and some missing obligations, and the research queue produced two new research files. Later snapshots still had retired tasks, a marked duplicate, and a summary that disagreed with its own category counts. Everything ran while I was in the conversation. The scheduled checks, text intake, recurring audits, and arrival concierge I had planned were never built.
